@@@ problem 228 summary-ranges
title: Summary Ranges
difficulty: Easy
topics: Array
params: nums
time: O(n)
space: O(1) extra (output excluded)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=n

=== description
You are given a **sorted** array `nums` of **unique** integers. Return the smallest sorted list of ranges that covers all the numbers exactly.

Format each range `[a, b]` as `"a->b"` if `a != b`, or `"a"` if `a == b`.

=== constraints
- `0 <= nums.length <= 20`
- `-2^31 <= nums[i] <= 2^31 - 1`
- `nums` is sorted in ascending order and all values are unique

=== hints
- A range continues while `nums[i+1] == nums[i] + 1`.
- Careful: `nums[i] + 1` can overflow for `INT_MAX`; compare using `long long` (or subtract instead).

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    vector<string> summaryRanges(vector<int>& nums) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::summaryRanges);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        std::vector<int> v;
        long long x = g.coin(0.2) ? (long long)INT_MIN : g.irange(-1000, 1000);
        for (int i = 0; i < n && x <= INT_MAX; ++i) {
            v.push_back((int)x);
            x += g.coin(0.6) ? 1 : g.irange(2, 5);
        }
        return lc::lines(v);
    });
}

=== approach
Walk the array with a pointer `i` at the start of a range. Extend `j` while the next number is exactly one larger. The range is `nums[i]..nums[j]`; output `"a"` or `"a->b"` and continue from `j + 1`. Using `long long` for the "+1" comparison avoids overflow at `INT_MAX`.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    vector<string> summaryRanges(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<string> out;
        int n = nums.size();
        for (int i = 0; i < n;) {
            int j = i;
            // extend while consecutive (long long avoids overflow at INT_MAX)
            while (j + 1 < n && (long long)nums[j + 1] == (long long)nums[j] + 1) ++j;
            if (i == j) out.push_back(to_string(nums[i]));
            else out.push_back(to_string(nums[i]) + "->" + to_string(nums[j]));
            i = j + 1;
        }
        return out;
    }
};

=== examples
[0,1,2,4,5,7]
> ["0->2","4->5","7"]

[0,2,3,4,6,8,9]
> ["0","2->4","6","8->9"]

=== tests
[]
> ?

[-1]
> ?

[-2147483648,-2147483647,2147483647]
> ?

[1,3,5,7]
> ?

@@@ problem 56 merge-intervals
title: Merge Intervals
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Sorting
params: intervals
time: O(n log n)
space: O(n)
bench: n=64..524288 time=nlogn space=n

=== description
Given an array of `intervals` where `intervals[i] = [start_i, end_i]`, merge all **overlapping** intervals and return the resulting non-overlapping intervals that cover all the input intervals.

Intervals that merely touch (like `[1,4]` and `[4,5]`) also overlap. The judge sorts your output, so any order is accepted.

=== constraints
- `1 <= intervals.length <= 10^4`
- `0 <= start_i <= end_i <= 10^4`

=== hints
- Sort by start. Then overlapping intervals are adjacent.
- Compare each interval with the **last merged** one.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> merge(vector<vector<int>>& intervals) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::merge).sort_output();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        std::vector<std::vector<int>> v;
        int span = std::max(10, 4 * n);
        for (int i = 0; i < std::max(n, 1); ++i) {
            int a = g.irange(0, span), len = g.irange(0, 6);
            v.push_back({a, a + len});
        }
        return lc::lines(v);
    });
}

=== approach
**Sort by start.** Then scan: if the current interval starts at or before the end of the last merged interval, they overlap, so extend the last one's end to the max of both ends. Otherwise start a new merged interval.

After sorting, any interval that overlaps the current merged block must come right after it, so one pass suffices. Sorting dominates: O(n log n).

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> merge(vector<vector<int>>& intervals) {
        sort(intervals.begin(), intervals.end());          // by start (then end)
        vector<vector<int>> merged;
        for (auto &iv : intervals) {
            if (!merged.empty() && iv[0] <= merged.back()[1]) {
                merged.back()[1] = max(merged.back()[1], iv[1]);   // overlap: extend
            } else {
                merged.push_back(iv);                               // gap: new block
            }
        }
        return merged;
    }
};

=== examples
[[1,3],[2,6],[8,10],[15,18]]
> [[1,6],[8,10],[15,18]]
: [1,3] and [2,6] overlap and become [1,6].

[[1,4],[4,5]]
> [[1,5]]
: Touching intervals are merged.

[[4,7],[1,4]]
> [[1,7]]

=== tests
[[1,1]]
> ?

[[1,4],[0,4]]
> ?

[[1,4],[2,3]]
> ?

[[2,3],[4,5],[6,7],[8,9],[1,10]]
> ?

@@@ problem 57 insert-interval
title: Insert Interval
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array
params: intervals, newInterval
time: O(n)
space: O(n)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=n

=== description
`intervals` is a list of **non-overlapping** intervals sorted by start. Insert `newInterval` into it, merging where necessary, so that the result is still sorted and non-overlapping. Return the new list.

=== constraints
- `0 <= intervals.length <= 10^4`
- `0 <= start_i <= end_i <= 10^5`
- `intervals` is sorted by `start_i` and non-overlapping
- `newInterval.length == 2`, `0 <= start <= end <= 10^5`

=== hints
- The result has three parts: intervals entirely before `newInterval`, those overlapping it, and those entirely after.
- Overlapping intervals are all absorbed into one: min of starts, max of ends.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> insert(vector<vector<int>>& intervals, vector<int>& newInterval) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::insert);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        std::vector<std::vector<int>> v;
        int x = g.irange(0, 3);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            int a = x + g.irange(0, 3), b = a + g.irange(0, 4);
            v.push_back({a, b});
            x = b + 1;
        }
        int a = g.irange(0, x + 2), b = a + g.irange(0, std::max(1, x / 4));
        return lc::lines(v, std::vector<int>{a, b});
    });
}

=== approach
Since the list is sorted and disjoint, one scan in three phases works:

1. copy intervals that **end before** `newInterval` starts;
2. **absorb** every interval that overlaps it, widening `newInterval` to the min start and max end, then add it;
3. copy the remaining intervals, which all start after it.

Each interval is handled once: O(n) time.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> insert(vector<vector<int>>& intervals, vector<int>& newInterval) {
        vector<vector<int>> out;
        int i = 0, n = intervals.size();
        int s = newInterval[0], e = newInterval[1];

        while (i < n && intervals[i][1] < s) out.push_back(intervals[i++]);   // 1) before
        while (i < n && intervals[i][0] <= e) {                               // 2) overlapping
            s = min(s, intervals[i][0]);
            e = max(e, intervals[i][1]);
            ++i;
        }
        out.push_back({s, e});
        while (i < n) out.push_back(intervals[i++]);                          // 3) after
        return out;
    }
};

=== examples
[[1,3],[6,9]]
[2,5]
> [[1,5],[6,9]]

[[1,2],[3,5],[6,7],[8,10],[12,16]]
[4,8]
> [[1,2],[3,10],[12,16]]
: [4,8] overlaps [3,5], [6,7] and [8,10].

=== tests
[]
[5,7]
> ?

[[1,5]]
[2,3]
> ?

[[1,5]]
[6,8]
> ?

[[3,5],[12,15]]
[6,6]
> ?

[[1,5]]
[0,0]
> ?

@@@ problem 452 minimum-number-of-arrows-to-burst-balloons
title: Minimum Number of Arrows to Burst Balloons
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Greedy, Sorting
params: points
time: O(n log n)
space: O(log n)
bench: n=64..524288 time=nlogn space=logn

=== description
Balloons are taped to a wall; balloon `i` spans the horizontal interval `points[i] = [x_start, x_end]`. An arrow shot vertically at position `x` bursts every balloon with `x_start <= x <= x_end`.

Return the **minimum number of arrows** needed to burst all balloons.

=== constraints
- `1 <= points.length <= 10^5`
- `-2^31 <= x_start < x_end <= 2^31 - 1`

=== hints
- Sort balloons by their **end**.
- Shoot at the end of the first balloon; it bursts every balloon that starts before that point. Then repeat with the first balloon it missed.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int findMinArrowShots(vector<vector<int>>& points) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::findMinArrowShots);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        std::vector<std::vector<int>> v;
        bool extreme = g.coin(0.2);
        for (int i = 0; i < std::max(n, 1); ++i) {
            long long a = extreme ? g.range(INT_MIN, INT_MAX - 1) : g.range(-4 * n - 10, 4 * n + 10);
            long long b = extreme ? g.range(a + 1, INT_MAX) : a + g.range(1, 8);
            v.push_back({(int)a, (int)b});
        }
        return lc::lines(v);
    });
}

=== approach
**Greedy by end point.** Sort balloons by `x_end`. Shoot the first arrow at the end of the first balloon: that is the rightmost position that still bursts it, so it bursts as many later balloons as possible. Skip every balloon whose start is `<=` that position; the first balloon that starts after it needs a new arrow at its own end.

Compare with `<` / `<=` directly instead of subtracting, to avoid overflow on extreme coordinates.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int findMinArrowShots(vector<vector<int>>& points) {
        sort(points.begin(), points.end(),
             [](const vector<int> &a, const vector<int> &b) { return a[1] < b[1]; });
        int arrows = 1;
        int pos = points[0][1];                 // first arrow: end of first balloon
        for (size_t i = 1; i < points.size(); ++i) {
            if (points[i][0] > pos) {           // starts after the arrow: missed
                ++arrows;
                pos = points[i][1];
            }
        }
        return arrows;
    }
};

=== examples
[[10,16],[2,8],[1,6],[7,12]]
> 2
: Shoot at x = 6 (bursts [2,8] and [1,6]) and at x = 11 (bursts [10,16] and [7,12]).

[[1,2],[3,4],[5,6],[7,8]]
> 4

[[1,2],[2,3],[3,4],[4,5]]
> 2

=== tests
[[1,2]]
> ?

[[-2147483648,2147483647]]
> ?

[[-2147483646,-2147483645],[2147483646,2147483647]]
> ?

[[3,9],[7,12],[3,8],[6,8],[9,10],[2,9],[0,9],[3,9],[0,6],[2,8]]
> ?
